WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.759 --> 00:00:16.440
so I think my first visit is a bit of a
blur but it was a long time ago

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.510
it was definitely when I was a student
it would have been in the sort of mid

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:26.660
90s and I came as a student and I was
looking at all the architecture of

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:31.980
Cambridge and Boston and MIT and you
know very special pilgrimage to come to

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.710
this chapel because I'd seen it in
photographs it was incredibly

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.970
inspirational to me and my thesis work
so I wanted to finally see this building

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:43.830
that I understood sort of sectionally
and you know atmospherically it was

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.520
really special during my master's
program I was I became very interested

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.830
in working on a faith-based space and it
was one of the sort of thesis studio

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.760
projects and I looked at lots of
examples of which there's so many of

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.900
course around the world and I was
particularly struck by this little

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:07.409
chapel at MIT because it
proposed a section and what I mean by

00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:10.680
that is the kind of the slice through
the building the way in which it's

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.640
constructed that I'd never seen before
and I try to understand that and I read

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.880
what the intention was and I became
really fascinated by it why would you

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.390
spend so much energy and effort doing
something that was contrary to the way

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:29.820
in which most people would make a
building and when I came finally to the

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:35.100
building it it revealed its its purpose
very clearly because when you are here

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.930
with light and the water it's a very
powerful space and you understand the

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:47.870
Bertoia you understand the vessel you
understand the texture it all comes life

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:52.590
some of the particular features that
really struck me and made us a very

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:58.320
strong impression was a building which
used the elements to directly affect the

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.920
emotions of the function of the building
so you know when you normally make a

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.390
building it's you know you use materials
for performance functionality and

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:10.769
endurance you know all those things
aesthetics you know visual pleasure but

00:02:10.769 --> 00:02:15.569
I think here Saarinen did something more
he actually also marshaled the natural

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:20.500
elements to evoke a more powerful
emotional feeling

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:26.280
in the building by using water and by
creating a building which is hasn't a

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:32.590
relationship to the story you know it's
the sort of the notion of sort of a

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:36.970
Christian life it's this idea of a kind
of Arkham vessel so in a way literally

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:41.950
we're sitting in a vessel on a water
body and he makes he makes that analogy

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.400
really really close and I don't think I
never seen that in architecture before

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.660
so it was quite profound I love the
contrast between the outside and inside

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:53.410
of this building um I think for most
people from the outside it probably even

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:57.940
looks like a you know a power station or
something but I mean I think it's it's

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:04.260
clearly not it's more than that it is a
very astute port portioned sort of

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:09.550
gentle full stop in the landscape it's
almost like putting a perfect dot at the

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:15.850
right point and it understands
that that's all it needs to do and in a

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:20.200
in a space which in a way could almost
be multi-denominational it really is

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.489
let's base there yes it was designed as
a chapel a Christian space but it's really

00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:30.790
at almost a kind of multi-faith space in
my mind he's very careful to to put in

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:34.750
signature but also to create a kind of a
notion of a different kind of building

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:38.500
that's being celebrated here which is
different to an academic building or a

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:43.120
campus building so it's very close on
the outside it really speaks about the

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.430
inner world not the outer world and I
think that even if you don't know the

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.700
out language of architecture I think
intuitively that's there and then you

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:56.230
really you find yourself elevated on
this bridge the you know the walkway is

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:00.040
not through a standard door that you
come up to there's this pond that float

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.790
that's sort of around it and you look at
the water and you look at the pond and

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.820
then you and I think you have to bridge
into it so you you really have to take a

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:12.250
journey from the outside to the inside
and it uses all the devices that make

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:16.440
that narrative clear
and you know when you come in it's you

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:22.500
are not disappointed the inner life of
the building is extremely rich it's

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:29.850
varied its emotional it's inspiring yeah
I think what Saarinen is really trying

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:35.490
to do is to create a very particular
counterpoint to the campus generally and

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:39.300
he's making a really beautiful full stop
what I call a full stop

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.210
you know I think the importance of a
full stop in a sentence is really

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.880
important and if the campus is the
sentence this is the full stop that

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.150
makes you pause now what I mean by that
is that it's a kind of proportion

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:57.930
geometry sort of pile of brick that has
a very particular position and and in

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:03.630
way its austere on the outside but I
think that austerity speaks to something

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.390
that Saarinen understood which was that
there's a bigger community than just the

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.660
Christian community here and I
think he was trying to signify that this

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:14.430
is a faith space or a different kind of
space of space of the inner world rather

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:19.920
than the outer world and I think he
reinforces that even more I mean people

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.970
think it's castles and and such like but
I think it's it's really using

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:30.720
architectural sort of systems to really
speak to a way of making construction in

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.940
the 20th century that speaks to
traditions so this idea of making a

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:39.930
building which is literally hovering
above a body of water the water is a

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:43.200
pond of course and it's wonderful
from the outside as a reflecting system

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.820
but it activates the interior of the
building very dynamically and then

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:52.350
you come across not so much a moat
because that's kind of to do with

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:57.180
defense and security it's really in a
permanent bridge and it's this idea of

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:01.350
elevating and you know you sort of and
why is it a special bridge, it's you're

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:05.190
surrounded by this kind of colored glass
you know it's really this idea of moving

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:09.570
from the kind of spectral light of the
outside to a very kind of focused sort

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:14.820
of spectrum of light and color before
you enter the main chamber and you see

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:20.250
this incredible volume where almost like
a baroque Chapel you know the walls are

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:24.610
undulating with life
and you realize when

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:29.139
sit you're sitting in a chamber which is
disconnected from the from the exterior

00:06:29.139 --> 00:06:33.130
because suddenly there's light kind of
coming upwards light doesn't come up

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:40.810
light comes down so he's really
emotionally sort of channeling sort of

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:43.780
things that are not what you
traditionally find in a modernist

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:48.699
building and he's using narrative
narrative implicit within the notion of

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:52.090
faith that narrative implicit in the
idea of transitioning from an outer

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:56.020
world to an inner world to really make a
very powerful building just when you

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:01.449
think you think you understand how a
material can you know is perceived in

00:07:01.449 --> 00:07:05.740
the world I think a great artist or an
architect can take that very mundane

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:12.280
material and make it magical or let it
have another message and I think what

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:17.800
you're seeing in this Saarinen vessel is
the way in which rejected bricks you

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:22.930
know the things that are at fault I
brought to play in this volume in a

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:27.280
really profound way so they're rejected
bricks which would normally be thrown

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.849
away from this a brick foundry because
it's all about the perfection are the

00:07:30.849 --> 00:07:35.680
things that are celebrated and giving
articulation to the volume you know

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:41.159
instead of bringing in new technology to
make acoustics he articulates the

00:07:41.159 --> 00:07:47.919
brick skin to allow it to also absorb
sound and not be a kind of live space

00:07:47.919 --> 00:07:51.009
but to be a kind of really important
space that allows concentration of the

00:07:51.009 --> 00:07:54.849
mind so that the single material is
being asked to do a lot of things both

00:07:54.849 --> 00:08:01.000
functionally and poetically and
metaphorically and it transcends it's

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.680
very kind of mundane nature and I know
that's a very special quality the

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:13.150
atmosphere of the of the of the chapel
is articulated in a very powerful

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:17.320
and precise way it's a reduction of
material so what you just see is the

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:23.680
brick and and then the wooden vessel
sort of the banister that wraps around

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:27.240
at the
level and I think the wooden banister is

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:32.250
the metaphor of the boat so immediately
this whole notion of being entering the

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:37.050
vessel is articulated by this banister
and it's disconnection from the brick

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:41.340
which is the world in a way and then
really this notion of how light is

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:47.640
articulated is very powerful around
you the light comes from below you sort

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:53.940
of reflected reflected but at the altar
it's a it's a very singular sort of Zen

00:08:53.940 --> 00:09:00.150
sort of focus almost like a
spotlight on the on the on the on what

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:04.530
the minds focus is supposed to be and
it's the only daylight that's allowed to

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:09.740
sort of stream from above and and it's a
kind of wonderful moment where Bertoia's

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:14.070
sort of commission Bertoia's commission
to make an extraordinary sculpture and I

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:18.450
hear the sculptures purpose is really to
slow down the like right it's sort of

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:22.800
just to make it visible to the eye I
mean of course it's visible but there's

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:27.030
something about the way in which the
petals of pretoria sculpture capture

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:31.200
almost as it were fragments of the light
and sort of hold it hold it still for

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.550
your eyes to gaze upon does this
incredible thing of slowing it down

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:38.910
almost making it look like it is
descending like a shower that's a very

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:43.260
powerful thing so that's what you have
is this building made out of brick wood

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:47.940
and light yeah I think Saarinen is
really careful to really understand the

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:52.110
nature of a sacred building and a
profane building and I think that that's

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:56.850
the duality that he really puts out in
the middle of this campus then he talks

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:00.440
about being able to understand it near
the nature of the sacred and profane and

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:06.180
in a way this is the sacred vessel which
is the intimate space and the space of

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:10.860
debate and discussion is the Kresge Hall and that is more like a

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:15.480
tent right it's more like a stretched
tent a gathering space for everybody to

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.380
come to it's multi-directional there's
no front or back really I mean there are

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:24.210
doors in one place but the idea is that
visually it's multi-directional and in

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:28.710
the heart of it is this great auditorium
which collects and gathers people it's

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:32.040
made very much with the same kind of
materials as break-ins and timber but it

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:35.880
has obviously the copper on the outside
but I think this idea of the sort of

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:41.040
chamber and the tent are this sort of
dialectic that Saarinen gives as a gift to

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:45.060
the MIT campus to talk about this
duality and it's very poetically very

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:49.079
powerful I think the chapel of course
has a relationship to the brick of

00:10:49.079 --> 00:10:52.800
Boston and I think Saarinen is very
clear about that but I don't think he's

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:57.180
trying to make sort of memetic
references or or anything like that I

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:00.930
think he he uses brick because brick is
simply the humble material it's the

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:04.949
mundane material and I think he's very
interested in how does he take a mundane

00:11:04.949 --> 00:11:07.980
material and make it transmit
transfigures that literally transfigures

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:14.130
it with with nature with light and with
geometry and proportion so I think that

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:19.170
you know it's meant to be singular but
from a contextual point of view you can

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:22.920
also see how it completely fits within
the Boston landscape within the MIT

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:28.560
campus I think that it's it's always an
important charge for an architect or an

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:34.829
artist or designer to be able to use any
material and to not see inherent in the

00:11:34.829 --> 00:11:40.889
material limits but to see in any
material the possibilities so for me I'm

00:11:40.889 --> 00:11:46.740
you know what Saarinen does with brick I'm very inspired by in my own work in that

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:50.310
I'm very interested in using materials
that you just disregard in your mind's

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:54.000
eye is not being precious or valuable
and I'm interested in reconfiguring

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:00.569
those to represent the value of that not
to be sensational or to just just to be

00:12:00.569 --> 00:12:03.839
you know just to be alternative or
anything like that but just to really

00:12:03.839 --> 00:12:07.889
talk about the preciousness of matter I
mean that's kind of I think the deep

00:12:07.889 --> 00:12:12.029
sort of thing behind it is that this is
got a deep respect for matter on this

00:12:12.029 --> 00:12:16.680
planet and things that are made things
there from the planet formed or reformed

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:21.839
sort of and this idea that everything is
precious and not about a kind of certain

00:12:21.839 --> 00:12:25.709
hierarchy you know a certain stone is
not more precious than you know

00:12:25.709 --> 00:12:30.089
composite for me or you know etc etc
it's all valuable and all should be

00:12:30.089 --> 00:12:32.639
given the kind of dignity of being able
to transfigure into something that

00:12:32.639 --> 00:12:36.990
inspires I think that the urban planning
and

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:44.610
architectural departments at MIT are
very lucky to have a gift these two

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:51.210
buildings really present this sort of
some of the key components of our of our

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:55.830
societies in the sense of you know are
introverted world's the sort of secular

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:59.820
and the the sort of faith-based world
and the public world you know and I

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:04.860
think Saarinen gives a gift to MIT and
to generations of architects you know

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.880
and he makes two timeless structures
that are really going to be

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.720
inspirational for generations to come I
think these two buildings as we move

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.080
into the centuries that are coming are
going to become even more powerful as

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:21.390
icons about you know how we make
buildings the idea that the chapel is

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:26.480
here on the MIT campus which is a kind
of science and technical based sort of

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:34.020
institution really speaks to this notion
of understanding the spirit in in

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:37.940
science that there's a kind of there's
also a kind of you know this notion of a

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:43.560
of a spiritual space or spirituality is
not about unknown things it's also about

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:50.550
the kind of wonder of the world as well
and to be to be ever present and aware

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:55.350
and then or about how beautiful it is
and how powerful of this and I think

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:59.700
that the chapel creates that
counterpoint to the sort of searching

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:03.900
research which is about uncovering the
world but also a space to kind of

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:09.300
reflect and to to take in the world take
him in the kind of vastness of the world

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:13.320
and it also in a way to understand that
there's a lot done but there's also so

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:19.280
much to do it's sort of a wonderful it's
a wonderful meditative chamber

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:26.779
 

